# agent-sandbox environment

## Running commands
{{if .Hook}}- Run shell commands with your normal Bash/Monitor tools; a PreToolUse hook
  transparently routes each one through the command broker.
- Command output is returned inline in the tool result.
{{else}}- The Bash and Monitor tools are disabled; use the `run_command` MCP tool.
- Command output is written to files; read the returned stdout/stderr paths.
{{end}}
There is no shell in the loop. The broker parses your command line itself
(pipelines, `&&`/`||`/`;`, redirections, globbing, `$(…)`, `for`/`if`, `cd` and
the other shell builtins all work) and executes each simple command directly —
never implicitly through `bash` or `sh` (invoking either yourself is a
different thing — see below). Builtins run inside the broker itself, at the
broker's own grants, so a redirect or glob you write is bounded by those, not by
whichever command's output it is attached to.

## Where the boundary is
Some commands run in their own child sandbox with their own filesystem, network
and argv grants; the rest run at the broker's own grants. Which is which is
decided by `{{.ProfilePath}}`, and this document deliberately does not copy that
file — ask nono, which resolves groups, `~`, `$WORKDIR` and `@git:` tokens that
reading the JSON cannot:

- `nono why --profile {{.ProfilePath}} --command <name>` — is this command
  policy-controlled, and may I invoke it? Add `--caller <name>` to ask about a
  chained edge rather than your own.
- `nono why --profile {{.ProfilePath}} --path <p> --op read|write` (`--host`
  for network) answers for the **broker's own sandbox**, not a policy
  command's. `--command` and `--path` do not combine: pass both and `--path`
  is silently dropped, so this answers the command question instead — it
  cannot tell you what a policy-controlled command's own child sandbox can
  reach.
- `nono profile show --json {{.ProfilePath}}` — the full resolved profile,
  including each policy command's own sandbox under `command_policies` (its
  `from.<caller>.sandbox`). The plain form without `--json` only summarises:
  it lists policy-controlled command names, nothing about what any of them
  can reach.

`sh` and `bash` are themselves declared commands with their own child sandbox —
reachable from the session, confined to the working directory's own data with
no network, though able to run tools out of the nix store and the mise
toolchains. There is no `nono why --path` route into a child sandbox's own
grants (see above); read `nono profile show --json` for either one's
specifics rather than assume it shares the session's own reach.

Neither command reports environment variables: `nono profile show` does not print
`environment.allow_vars`. If a variable is not arriving, read that list in the
file itself. `agent-sandbox doctor` measures the one that matters most,
`AGENT_SANDBOX_BROKER_SOCKET`, without which nothing reaches the broker at all.

## Reading a failure
- **Exit 126 with a reason naming `command_policies`** — a policy denial. The
  reason names the exact edge. Do not retry; ask the operator to change the
  profile.
- **Exit 126 saying the shim `failed to connect to … supervisor.sock`** — not a
  policy denial. A policy-controlled command invoked from deep inside nested
  shell scripts can fail this way. Re-running the command directly, rather than
  through the nesting, is worth one attempt.
- **Exit 127, or `Permission denied` from the program itself** — the path, host
  or binary is outside what the profile grants. Not a rule to phrase around.
- **`agent-sandbox: refused: this pipeline pipes two or more policy-controlled
  commands together …`, exit 126** — not a nono denial; the broker refused it
  itself, before running anything, because two policy-controlled commands
  (`git`, `ssh`, `bash`, `sh`) were piped directly together. Run them as
  separate commands instead.
- **`fatal: cannot exec '.git/hooks/commit-msg': Permission denied`** (or any
  other `.git/hooks/*`) — not a policy denial. Git hooks do not execute inside
  this sandbox at all; use `git commit --no-verify`. Because of that,
  commit-message validation does not run inside the sandbox — no hook is going
  to catch a malformed message, so get it right yourself before committing.
  This project follows Conventional Commits; see `.claude/rules/git-commit.md`.

A toolchain that can compile and immediately execute code is bounded only by
what its own sandbox reaches — not by argv rules, and not by which other tools
are declared. Ask what its grants are before assuming otherwise.

## Changing the config
`{{.ConfigPath}}` names two nono profiles; you may edit all three files.

- Your own sandbox — this process, its file tools, and any MCP server it spawns
  as a direct child — is `{{.AgentProfilePath}}`.
- The sandbox every shell command runs in is `{{.ProfilePath}}`.

Both are written in nono's own schema. agent-sandbox neither generates nor reads
either one; it hands each path to nono.

Editing **does not affect the current session**: both profiles are read once, at
session start. An edit takes effect the next time the operator runs
`agent-sandbox claude`. After editing, run `agent-sandbox ai config-check` — it
hands both profiles to `nono profile validate`. `agent-sandbox doctor` goes
further: it adds the broker-socket probe, asks nono whether the command profile
leaves the broker's own binary writable, and checks that the paths the profile
pins still exist on this host.

Schema:

```toml
tool_mode = "hook"                       # "hook" | "mcp"
command_profile = "command-profile.json" # default name; shared by every agent

[mcp]
command_output_dir = "..."               # mcp tool_mode only: where command output is written

[agents.claude]
profile = "claude-profile.json"          # default name: "<agent>-profile.json"
```

Both profile paths resolve beside `{{.ConfigPath}}` unless written absolute.

A user-scope config at `~/.config/agent-sandbox/config.toml` is composed with
this file: every value the project file sets wins, and an agent the user-scope
file declares alone still applies. If a profile path you did not write shows up
in `agent-sandbox ai config-check`, it comes from there.
